From c51c641c83158d249f9f7316475be4fdaed6814e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dtingley11 <l0tsofcub3s@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:29:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] sched/cass: Introduce the Capacity Aware Superset
 Scheduler (CASS)

Adds the CASS implementation, which optimizes runqueue selection of CFS
and RT tasks. By using CPU capacity as a basis for comparing the relative
utilization between different CPUs, CASS fairly balances load across CPUs
of varying capacities. This results in improved multi-core performance,
especially when CPUs are overutilized because CASS does not clip a CPU's
utilization when it eclipses the CPU's capacity.

As a superset of capacity aware scheduling, CASS implements a hierarchy
of criteria to determine the better CPU to wake a task upon between CPUs
that have the same relative utilization. This way, single-core performance,
latency, and cache affinity are all optimized where possible.

CASS does not feature explicit energy awareness, but its basic load
balancing principle results in decreased overall energy, often better
than what is possible with explicit energy awareness.

Author: Sultan Alsawaf <sultan@kerneltoast.com>. The 2023-2025 copyright
attribution is preserved in the source files.

Provenance: the CASS implementation was originally introduced by Sultan
Alsawaf (dev-sm8350 r13 release notes; introduction commit 06398a6) and
followed by a series of CASS fixes. This port is based on the CASS
as it exists in two authentic vendor trees that carry the full,
most-recently-fixed CASS:

  - kernel_oneplus_sm8350 (dev-sm8350 15.2, CASS with
    CONFIG_SCHED_WALT / non-WALT variants, 2023-2024 copyright), and
  - the android13-5.15-lts kernel (ChicKernel gemstones device kernel
    repository, branch android13-5.15-lts, tip 465df0508d2c). In that
    tree CASS was introduced in 41b7987d0ec3, reverted in
    ac1dbabcb860, re-introduced in 7da97316eae8 (with the fix series),
    and then refined by, in order: 611c88f07bee (Fix CPU selection when
    no candidate CPUs are idle), fe1eddbd43ff (Clean up local variable
    scope in cass_best_cpu()), a7d660d78b21 (Perform runqueue selection
    for RT tasks too), 4b96c278457e (Fix suboptimal task placement when
    uclamp is used), 724f61381950 (Only treat sync waker CPU as idle if
    there's one task running), 0941b7cc3366 (Eliminate redundant calls
    to smp_processor_id()), 896f44360aed (Fix disproportionate load
    spreading when CPUs are throttled), 410c7d8b3502 (Honor uclamp even
    when no CPUs can satisfy the requirement), 222881a2536d (Don't fight
    the idle load balancer), and 5b2d929f37e3 (Don't pack tasks with
    uclamp boosts below minimum CPU capacity).

This tree (307 lines of CASS core) is the most recently fixed authentic
CASS; it is used as the porting base for this GKI 5.15 kernel.

Port change: the idle-preference check in cass_best_cpu() used
arch_scale_min_freq_capacity(cpu), which is not provided by this GKI 5.15
tree. It is replaced with arch_scale_cpu_capacity(cpu), comparing the
task's uclamp floor against the CPU's full (maximum-frequency) capacity
instead of its minimum-frequency capacity. This slightly favors idle CPUs
over the reference behavior in edge cases where a uclamp-boosted task
only fits at a CPU's higher frequencies.
